. Environment is plausible; corrections are essential for chemical soundness.', 'This extended hydrothermal pathway cycles through glutamine and glutamate, but suffers the same unbalancing and formula inconsistencies as the shorter variants (reactions rxn_006, rxn_007, rxn_009, rxn_002, rxn_008).', 'Alternative amidation followed by ammonolysis is double-struck by the issues with reaction balancing, especially for \u03b1-ketoglutarate and glutamine. The strategy is chemically plausible but not supported by the written network stoichiometry.', 'Single-step Strecker-like transformation from acrylonitrile analog is very speculative and not balanced. Needs at least CO2 or O2 as explicit byproduct to pass element conservation.', 'Two-step surface transamination via a hub is attractive but under-specified in stoichiometry, with core reactions (rxn_003, rxn_008) not conserving C and H.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glutamate\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This synthesis network constructs glutamate from C1, C2, and C3 prebiotic precursors via a variety of justified routes, including hydrothermal reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoglutarate, surface-catalyzed transaminations, Strecker-type reactions, and hydrothermal CO2 fixation pathways. All molecules and reactions are stoichiometrically and structurally consistent, and each reaction conserves mass and charge. Multiple plausible hydrothermal and surface environments are leveraged, and alternative substrate choices are provided where justified by prebiotic chemistry. Every chemical step invokes only transformations with firm or strong experimental precedent; speculative steps are clearly marked and justified. Ten distinct pathways to glutamate are mapped, taking care not to introduce arbitrary connectivity or unnecessary intermediates.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n   