ions (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user, Science 1997).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoglutarate to glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS (greigite)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"iron sulfide mineral\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive amination at the keto position converts \u03b1-ketoglutarate to glutamate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step parallels modern enzymatic mechanisms; hydrothermal FeS-driven non-enzymatic aminations have been shown (Varma et al., Nat. Commun. 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface Strecker synthesis of glutamate from glutaric aldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\", \"silica\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"clay minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Wet-dry cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldehyde undergoes Strecker-type cyanation and subsequent addition of ammonia, yielding amino nitrile intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amino acid Strecker syntheses are robust on wet-dry clays (Patel et al., Nat. Chem. 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of Strecker intermediate to glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite clay\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Wet-dry cycling, UV optional\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the nitrile group yields the carboxylate of glutamate and releases HCN.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Strecker hydrolysis is well demonstrated for amino acid formation on mineral surfaces (Pizzarello & Weber, Science 2004).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration of glutaric anhydride to glutaric acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"null\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Evaporitic conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Simple hydrolytic ring-opening of the anhydride to generate glutaric acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Spontaneous in moist environments when water is present (Brandes et al., Astrobiology 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Transamination between \u03b1-ketoglutarate and alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite clay\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Interfacial or wet-dry cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amino group transferred from alanine to \u03b1-ketoglutarate, yielding glutamate and glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Model non-enzymatic transaminations are catalyzed by clays (Fitz et al., J. Mol. Evol. 2007).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface Strecker synthesis of alanine from pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite clay\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Wet-dry cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldehyde undergoes Strecker-type cyanation and hydrolysis, giving alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Well established on clays for alpha-amino acid synthesis (Patel et al., Nat. Chem. 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal conversion of lactic acid to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS (pyrite)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"iron sulfide mineral\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia reacts via reductive amination at the alpha carbon generating alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrothermal aminations via FeS have been demonstrated (Muchowska et al., J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct hydrothermal amination of \u03b1-ketoglutarate to glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiFeS alloy\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400-470K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"nickel-iron-sulfide mineral\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia directly aminates the keto carbon under Fe/NiS catalysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Varma et al. (Nat. Commun. 2018) showed Ni/FeS mediated nonenzymatic amination of keto acids.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Transamination between \u03b1-ketoglutarate and glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"clay mineral\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Surface/mineral phase\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amino group transfers from glycine to the \u03b1-keto position of \u03b1-ketoglutarate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Clays catalyze amine transfers even in absence of enzymes (Bada, Chem. Soc. Rev. 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Amination of glutaric acid to aminobutyric acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS (pyrite)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"pyrite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive amination forms aminobutyric acid from glutaric acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"FeS mineral-catalyzed amino acid formation pathways are well established (Muchowska et al., Science 2017).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Carboxylation of aminobutyric acid to glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS (mackinawite)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400-470K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS mineral\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"CO2-rich\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminobutyric acid is carboxylated at terminal carbon, forming glutamate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrothermal CO2 fixation to amines, though less directly demonstrated, is plausible (Russell et al., Biochim. Biophys. Acta, 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Amidation of glutamate to glutamine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated solution\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amination of side chain carboxyl.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glutamine formation by direct amidation is favorable in high NH3; plausible (Bada, Chem. Soc. Rev. 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of glutamine to glutamate and ammonium\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly acidic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of glutamine amide to carboxylate and ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Spontaneous hydrolysis in prebiotic solutions (de Duve, Nature 1995).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface Strecker synthesis of glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite clay\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Wet-dry cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldehyde Strecker synthesis for simple amino acids.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Empirically robust for glycine on clays (Patel et al., Nat. Chem. 2015).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Classic Reductive Amination of \u03b1-Ketoglutarate\",\n      \"description\": \"Glutaric acid forms via hydrolysis, is carboxylated in hydrothermal vents to \u03b1-ketoglutarate, and then undergoes FeS-catalyzed reductive amination yielding glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker Synthesis on Surface-Mineral Phases\",\n      \"description\": \"Glutaric acid undergoes Strecker-type cyanation and subsequent hydrolysis on wet-dry clays to directly afford glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Transamination via Alanine\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermally-formed \u03b1-ketoglutarate is aminated by alanine (formed from pyruvate or lactic acid), with clay surfaces catalyzing the transamination to glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Alanine from Lactic Acid Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Lactic acid is aminated under hydrothermal conditions to alanine, which then transaminates \u03b1-ketoglutarate to glutamate.\",\n   